# RISC0 v1.2.1 Metrics Standards

## Context
- When proving computations with RISC0 v1.2.1
- When monitoring performance and resource usage
- When debugging proof generation issues

## Requirements
- Always collect and emit session statistics from ProveInfo
- Track both cycle counts and proof sizes
- Calculate and log percentage breakdowns of cycle types
- Monitor segment counts for multi-segment proofs
- Track seal and journal sizes for proof storage requirements
- Use tracing or logging framework consistently

## Examples

<example>
```rust
use tracing;

fn emit_proof_metrics(info: ProveInfo) -> Result<()> {
    // Basic session statistics
    tracing::info!("Session segments: {}", info.stats.segments);
    
    // Cycle counts
    tracing::info!("Total cycles: {}", info.stats.total_cycles);
    
    // Calculate percentages
    let pct = |cycles: u64| {
        cycles as f64 / info.stats.total_cycles as f64 * 100.0
    };
    
    tracing::info!(
        "User cycles: {} ({:.2}%)", 
        info.stats.user_cycles,
        pct(info.stats.user_cycles)
    );
    
    tracing::info!(
        "Paging cycles: {} ({:.2}%)", 
        info.stats.paging_cycles,
        pct(info.stats.paging_cycles)
    );
    
    tracing::info!(
        "Reserved cycles: {} ({:.2}%)", 
        info.stats.reserved_cycles,
        pct(info.stats.reserved_cycles)
    );
    
    // Proof sizes
    tracing::info!(
        "Journal size: {} bytes", 
        info.receipt.journal.len()
    );
    
    // Handle different receipt types correctly
    match &info.receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) => {
            tracing::info!(
                "Composite seal size: {} bytes", 
                cr.seal_size()  // Use seal_size() for composite receipts
            );
        }
        InnerReceipt::Succinct(sr) => {
            tracing::info!(
                "Succinct seal size: {} bytes", 
                sr.seal_size()
            );
        }
        InnerReceipt::Groth16(gr) => {
            tracing::info!(
                "Groth16 seal size: {} bytes", 
                gr.seal_size()
            );
        }
        InnerReceipt::Fake(_) => {
            tracing::info!("Fake receipt (no seal)");
        }
    }
    
    Ok(())
}
```

Usage in proving workflow:
```rust
let prover = get_prover_server(&ProverOpts::default())?;
let prove_info = prover.prove_session(&ctx, &session)?;
emit_proof_metrics(prove_info)?;
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```rust
// DON'T: Incorrect seal size access
fn wrong_seal_access(info: ProveInfo) {
    if let InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) = &info.receipt.inner {
        println!("Size: {}", cr.get_seal_bytes().len());  // ❌ Wrong - get_seal_bytes() doesn't exist
    }
}

// DON'T: Missing receipt type handling
fn incomplete_metrics(info: ProveInfo) {
    println!("Total cycles: {}", info.stats.total_cycles);
    // Missing cycle breakdowns, segment count, and proof sizes
}

// DON'T: Inconsistent logging
fn mixed_logging(info: ProveInfo) {
    tracing::info!("Cycles: {}", info.stats.total_cycles);
    println!("Segments: {}", info.stats.segments); // Use tracing consistently
}
```
</example>

## Critical Notes
- Always verify that total_cycles = user_cycles + paging_cycles + reserved_cycles
- Monitor segment count to detect unexpected proof splitting
- Track seal and journal sizes to manage storage requirements
- Use consistent logging framework throughout the codebase
- For CompositeReceipt, always use seal_size() to get total seal size, never try to access seal bytes directly
- Different receipt types (Composite, Succinct, Groth16) have different ways to access their seals - always match on InnerReceipt type 